#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec0bb55c-2478-5ca6-9028-dffe1f54ee0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304361} {:answer/id #uuid "5d28b00f-c81d-5afa-ba9c-f2ad62354a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304362} {:answer/id #uuid "64f21bfd-b485-5a05-b79a-1e1631fc9c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304363} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6465f0-6fc0-501d-8a6d-7b2c2314438a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304364} {:answer/id #uuid "92607e7f-42a5-5e12-b3be-a47ebe07d54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec0bb55c-2478-5ca6-9028-dffe1f54ee0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304361} :answer-field/id #uuid "8643d4b8-5cd2-53f4-b852-10ae6e6938e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48a333df-65f2-5d7d-8030-7c0c9b8d850c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89872" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(-1,-1)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,0)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-2(0) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00318461-d584-5b79-8cb2-d4db81436019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331090} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa8e7e2-e5e2-5858-be5e-822390946ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331091} {:answer/id #uuid "c5df7c42-2476-5c73-b111-e2ae40cd4523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331092} {:answer/id #uuid "0fab083c-880e-5970-975a-d44019d64402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331093} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb095b5-1f19-53e0-9c80-f68032971793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aa8e7e2-e5e2-5858-be5e-822390946ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331091} :answer-field/id #uuid "40d1ca0f-9023-5861-a019-e6a4b20f6814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53528337-03e6-5063-9c35-a289ac5e1733" :question/math-academy-id "q-89877" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(-1,0)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+{e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =(y+{e}^{x})+{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y+2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,0)} & ={(y+2{e}^{x})|}_{(-1,0)} \\\\  & =0+2{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9f437e8-d7a0-55e0-9c33-0e0135821329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304368} {:answer/id #uuid "e131f356-600e-52cb-9a00-3d89e690dfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304369} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cf89ea-0d2e-5d6f-9a5d-68d65c26bedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304370} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf64a37-296e-54a0-8bc4-c5718135cea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304371} {:answer/id #uuid "aac9ad47-ff53-574a-a0c4-fcacc4efe232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9cf89ea-0d2e-5d6f-9a5d-68d65c26bedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304370} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb94a3fc-0359-5de2-852e-8b9aa0a90970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6677f8cd-6394-55a9-96b4-fd5ec63416b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89878" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =9(1)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c25a3ebb-d712-51a0-9ef7-0173c50102ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331097} {:answer/id #uuid "eabe37c3-ef66-56f9-b19b-a528f37c140a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331098} {:answer/id #uuid "0e86a70c-2ef9-549d-b428-2dbd8d5279b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331099} {:answer/id #uuid "0af3adb6-1511-568e-9518-6bf634e085b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331100} {:answer/id #uuid "94fbe1e3-180d-59a4-a78b-8b4b296aa5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eabe37c3-ef66-56f9-b19b-a528f37c140a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331098} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3fcb6d6-38d4-592e-977d-eb0c4153e39c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bddc166-b2f0-55f1-a976-9d329862898d" :question/math-academy-id "q-89886" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\  & =(y-x)-1 \\\\  & =y-x-1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x-1)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fed94e2f-457a-5498-ae8c-02cd095a9d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304375} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc0d378-80f9-53aa-87f8-d845fe6e635b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304376} {:answer/id #uuid "c419eea4-21e6-5e7c-99d9-584faaa2732e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304377} {:answer/id #uuid "4da0ecbd-4b78-5f21-8f94-f38388c38228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304378} {:answer/id #uuid "49fe9932-fa59-52ef-b9c8-e6d80f9c8a47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc0d378-80f9-53aa-87f8-d845fe6e635b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304376} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd0b64a-188b-59f2-9461-9323707d8c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90858788-5d30-592b-98c9-91d40707ab53" :question/math-academy-id "q-89888" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
